<section class="blk">
  <h2 id="wikitree">WikiTree, opened for the first time</h2>
  <p class="dek">Asked on 13 September 2026 whether it had ever been looked at, this archive had to answer
    no &mdash; the word appears nowhere in the project. Thirteen Booyzen profiles were then read. It is a
    <b>user-contributed tree and therefore a lead and not a source</b>, exactly like the eleven above it
    in that table. But it differs from them in one way that matters: <b>profiles carry a Sources section,
    and several of these cite documents by reference.</b></p>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>The accounting below is deliberately two-directional, because a tree is only useful if you are
      willing to say where it beats you and where you beat it.</p>

    <h3>Where it is ahead of this archive</h3>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:15rem">What WikiTree holds</th><th>What this archive held</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><b>Heese, <em>Suid-Afrikaanse Geslagsregisters</em>, Deel 1, <span class="m">p. 331</span></b></td>
              <td><a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Question 0</a> named the published Geslagsregisters as the
                route left for generation seven and this page marked it <b>"Not yet followed"</b>. There was
                no page number. <b>Now there is one.</b></td></tr>
          <tr><td>David Schalk Booijsen married <b>Adriana Christina Viljoen, 3 November 1828, Cradock</b></td>
              <td>The wife, but not the marriage &mdash; no date and no place.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Henry James Booyzen <b>born 12 August 1887</b>; married Dorothea Regina Barry
                <b>17 June 1912 at Elliot</b></td>
              <td>"c. 1887", worked back from age 69 on a burial register, and "in 1912" with no day and no
                church.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Jacobus Nicolaas Booyzen married <b>Jacomina Jacoba Jansen van Rensburg, 12 February 1866,
                Middelburg</b></td>
              <td>Nothing. He was the eldest name on the 1863 notice and an estate number, with no wife at
                all. <em>Caution: the profile's own source line dates that marriage 1899, not 1866.</em></td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>Where this archive is ahead of it</h3>
    <div class="scroll">
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th style="width:15rem">What WikiTree holds</th><th>What this archive holds</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Hendrik Stefanus Booysen as a son of <b>Anna Maria van der Merwe</b> &mdash; while the
                profile's own prose hedges <em>"Elizabetha ? (Anna Maria ?) van der Merwe"</em></td>
              <td><b>Resolved.</b> His 1908 death notice, TAB estate 14380, names his mother
                <b>ELIZABETHA BOOYSEN, BORN VAN DER MERWE</b>. Another researcher reached the same
                ambiguity and left it standing in a question mark; the notice settles it.
                <a href={u("/documents/#hendrik-1908")}>The notice &rarr;</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Charlotta Elizabetha Dormehl, <b>1841&ndash;1902</b></td>
              <td>Born about October 1841 at <b>Graaff-Reinet</b>; died <b>6 February 1902 at Driefontein,
                Groot Marico</b>, with two recorded married surnames.</td></tr>
          <tr><td><em>Hendrik Stiphanis</em> Booyzen, 1830&ndash;1905, of Graaff-Reinet</td>
              <td><b>Not this family.</b> Son of Jan Hendrik Booysen and Cornelia Margaretha Voetzé &mdash;
                and the profile has him <b>married at the age of twelve</b>, which is the kind of thing a
                shared tree carries in public for years.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <h3>And one head-on contradiction</h3>
    <blockquote><b>WikiTree gives Petrus Jacobus Booysen a different father.</b></blockquote>
    <p>Its profile <span class="m">Booysen-267</span> is unmistakably our man &mdash; born before
      <b>21 September 1788</b>, died <b>1 September 1876</b>, the same two dates this archive carries. It
      makes his parents <b>JAN (Boijens) BOOYSEN and MAGDALENA DE BEER</b>. This archive makes them
      <b>GERTHARDUS LODEWIKUS BOOIJSEN and MAGDALENA DE BEER</b>, and not on the strength of a tree: the
      names are in <b>line 3 of his 1876 Rustenburg Sterfkennis</b>, No. 678, which this archive has read
      and imaged. Same mother, same son, two different husbands.</p>
    <p><b>The reason this is not simply a tree being wrong</b> is that this archive has already met that
      Jan. Question 0 records a near-miss it deliberately declined to adopt &mdash; <span class="m">KAB NCD
      2/1: 49</span>, a notarial will of 1793 for <b>"Jan Booysen, married to the late Magdalena de
      Beer"</b> &mdash; and set it aside as <em>"wrong husband's name"</em>. Another researcher, working
      independently, has put that same Jan in that same position.</p>
    <p><b>Nothing has been changed on the strength of it.</b> A document this archive has read outranks a
      tree, and the 1876 notice stands. But the disagreement is now recorded, it has a published book and
      an exact page to be settled against, and the 1793 will has stopped being a curiosity.
      <a href={u("/open-questions/")}>Question 0 &rarr;</a> ·
      <a href={u("/searched/")}>What was searched &rarr;</a></p>
  </div>
</section>

